            {
                "entry_id": "ac134be1-d289-3196-8d50-10183a1baa47",
                "word": "soûlographie",
                "pos": "noun",
                "lang_code": "fr",
                "forms": [
                    {
                        "form_id": "a1300cc3-7bad-4895-b477-0aa100266c7d",
                        "entry_id": "ac134be1-d289-3196-8d50-10183a1baa47",
                        "tags": [
                            "plural"
                        ],
                        "form": "soûlographies"
                    },
                    {
                        "form_id": "714eb057-60f1-4e57-9e3a-ebdd5159665e",
                        "entry_id": "ac134be1-d289-3196-8d50-10183a1baa47",
                        "tags": [
                            "orthographe rectifiée de 1990"
                        ],
                        "form": "soulographie"
                    }
                ],
                "senses": [
                    {
                        "sense_id": "9261d114-c56b-3811-aba2-90206fe0da73",
                        "entry_id": "ac134be1-d289-3196-8d50-10183a1baa47",
                        "glosses": [
                            "Ivrognerie."
                        ],
                        "tags": [
                            "dated",
                            "familiar"
                        ],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "f50c5403-fe2b-4194-aeb7-884a27d14f7a",
                                "sense_id": "9261d114-c56b-3811-aba2-90206fe0da73",
                                "text": "S’il avait peu de connaissances en haute typographie, en revanche il passait pour être extrêmement fort dans un art que les ouvriers ont plaisamment nommé la soûlographie, art bien estimé par le divin auteur du Pantagruel, mais dont la culture, persécutée par les sociétés dites de tempérance, est de jour en jour plus abandonnée."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "bcc113da-aec3-4078-8488-e03f01461b77",
                                "sense_id": "9261d114-c56b-3811-aba2-90206fe0da73",
                                "text": "Cette transformation du sentiment en intérêt personnel, ordinairement lente, tortueuse et hypocrite chez les gens bien élevés, fut rapide et directe chez le vieil Ours, qui montra combien la soûlographie rusée l’emportait sur la typographie instruite."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "71fc255b-e6b5-4be9-a5f0-a5a1ca91ff8b",
                                "sense_id": "9261d114-c56b-3811-aba2-90206fe0da73",
                                "text": "Parfaitement ! Moi, le plus sobre des hommes ! Couché au poste !… Pour cause de soûlographie !"
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "5e78cb48-fb95-4658-8f40-efd552479b0f",
                                "sense_id": "9261d114-c56b-3811-aba2-90206fe0da73",
                                "text": "Mais où Joseph triomphe, c’est dans la politique. Grâce à lui, le petit café, dont l’enseigne : « À l’armée française ! » brille sur tout le quartier, le jour, en grosses lettres d’or, le soir, en grosses lettres de feu, est maintenant le rendez-vous officiel des antisémites marquants et des plus bruyants patriotes de la ville. Ceux-ci viennent fraterniser là, dans des soûlographies héroïques, avec des sous-officiers de l’armée et des gradés de la marine."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "7ec3f3aa-f903-4ab7-8ed1-1998cb09b110",
                                "sense_id": "9261d114-c56b-3811-aba2-90206fe0da73",
                                "text": "Soûlographie passée, Bergues était rentré chez lui."
                            }
                        ]
                    }
                ],
                "translations": [
                    {
                        "translation_id": "b206ade6-e2ed-4715-ab64-44c2828dc3e2",
                        "source_entry_id": "ac134be1-d289-3196-8d50-10183a1baa47",
                        "source_sense_id": "9261d114-c56b-3811-aba2-90206fe0da73",
                        "target_lang_word": "drunkenness",
                        "target_lang_code": "en",
                        "target_sense_ids": [],
                        "cross_references": [
                            {
                                "target_word": "drunkenness",
                                "target_lang_code": "en",
                                "target_senses": [
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "f17732b2-78ee-3532-8571-21917930638e",
                                        "entry_id": "1042a7d3-6e4c-3b39-b4d7-74613176ba25",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "A state of being drunk."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [
                                            "uncountable",
                                            "usually"
                                        ],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "574bbc9e-455a-499d-8522-ff97ea27727f",
                                                "sense_id": "f17732b2-78ee-3532-8571-21917930638e",
                                                "text": "But presently the fumes of the wine rising to his head, he became helplessly drunk and his side-muscles and limbs relaxed and he swayed to and fro on my back. When I saw that he had lost his senses for drunkenness, I put my head to his legs and, loosing them from my neck, stooped down well-nigh to the ground and threw him at full length, […]"
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "52bde6e9-85ff-477c-b8fa-ff4bcbdef3ad",
                                                "sense_id": "f17732b2-78ee-3532-8571-21917930638e",
                                                "text": "The Englishman had a reputation throughout Europe for gluttony; it was said that overeating was the English vice, just as lust was the French vice and drunkenness the German vice. Some Englishmen became very fat, and were famous for being so. Henry VIII ate enormous meals, but as a young man he was slim, perhaps because he always took a great deal of physical exercise. By the time that he was forty-five he was suffering from painful ulcers in his leg which prevented him from riding or walking without the greatest difficulty; but though he ceased to take exercise, he ate as much as ever. He then became very fat."
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    }
                                ]
                            }
                        ]
                    },
                    {
                        "translation_id": "d5b02bb9-fe8c-4506-931c-423bb0d2734c",
                        "source_entry_id": "ac134be1-d289-3196-8d50-10183a1baa47",
                        "source_sense_id": "9261d114-c56b-3811-aba2-90206fe0da73",
